#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124173 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "a01560be-9023-593a-87d3-7412f3b208a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3362" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y) = 6x^2+2y^2 - 2xy^2$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (3,3)$." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = k\n$$\n\nat the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0 \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (3,3)\n$$\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathrm{\\nabla } \\, f ( x , y ) & = {f}_{x} ( x , y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} + {f}_{y} ( x , y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x} ( 6 {x}^{2} + 2 {y}^{2} - 2 x {y}^{2} ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y} ( 6 {x}^{2} + 2 {y}^{2} - 2 x {y}^{2} ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = ( 12 x - 2 {y}^{2} ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} + ( 4 y - 4 x y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathrm{\\nabla } \\, f ( 3 , 3 ) & = ( 12 ( 3 ) - 2 ( 3 {)}^{2} ) \\mathbf{i} + ( 4 ( 3 ) - 4 ( 3 ) ( 3 ) ) \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = 18 \\, \\mathbf{i} - 24 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = \\langle  18 , - 24 \\rangle\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the level curve that passes through the point\n\n$$\n(x_0,y_0)=(3,3)\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x_{0}, y_{0}) \\cdot \\langle x - x_{0}, y - y_{0}\\rangle  &= 0 \\\\\n\\nabla f(3, 3) \\cdot \\langle x - 3, y - 3\\rangle  &= 0 \\\\\n\\langle 18,-24\\rangle  \\cdot \\langle x - 3, y - 3\\rangle  &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can leave our answer in this form. Or, if we want to leave our final answer in Cartesian form, we expand the dot product and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n18(x - 3) - 24(y - 3) &= 0 \\\\\n18x - 24y + 18 &= 0 \\\\\n3x - 4y + 3 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1940/Source/Images/c5004ab213a6d5c7a2ffee17af5d78e1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124174 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "f6400ab7-3684-5f0d-b8f0-1c85b0b322ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-3363" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^2-3xy+4y=2$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "First, if we set\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2-3xy+4y\n$$\n\nour curve can be viewed as the level curve\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2\n$$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1) = f_y(1,1) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(1,1) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1)$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathbf{t} ( x , y ) & = \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y} ( {x}^{2} - 3 x y + 4 y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} - \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x} ( {x}^{2} - 3 x y + 4 y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = ( - 3 x + 4 ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} - ( 2 x - 3 y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathbf{t} ( 1 , 1 ) & = ( - 3 ( 1 ) + 4 ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} - ( 2 ( 1 ) - 3 ( 1 ) ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = 1 \\, \\mathbf{i} - ( - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf i +\\mathbf j$, as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1940/Source/Images/5aaa7192901cc929e9c7816c7f43aa6f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124175 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "c4ca2e8a-5a5b-54c6-983d-2f5e821084ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-3364" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $6xy+3y^2 =7+ y - 3x^2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6xy + 3y^{2} &= 7 + y - 3x^{2} \\\\\n6xy + 3y^{2} - y + 3x^{2} &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 6xy+3y^2 - y + 3x^2\n$$\n\nour curve can be viewed as the level curve\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=7\n$$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x,y) = f_y(x,y) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x,y) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathbf{t} ( x , y ) & = {f}_{y} ( x , y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{i} - {f}_{x} ( x , y ) \\text{ } \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y} ( 6 x y + 3 {y}^{2} - y + 3 {x}^{2} ) \\, \\mathbf{i} - \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x} ( 6 x y + 3 {y}^{2} - y + 3 {x}^{2} ) \\, \\mathbf{j} \\\\  & = ( 6 x + 6 y - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} - ( 6 y + 6 x ) \\, \\mathbf{j}\\end{array}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j}$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t \\cdot \\mathbf j = 0\n$$\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathbf{t} ( x , y ) \\cdot  \\mathbf{j} & = 0 \\\\ ( ( 6 x + 6 y - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} - ( 6 y + 6 x ) \\, \\mathbf{j} ) \\cdot  \\mathbf{j} & = 0 \\\\ - 6 y - 6 x & = 0 \\\\ y & = - x\\end{array}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6xy + 3y^{2} &= 7 + y - 3x^{2} \\\\\n6x(-x) + 3(-x)^{2} &= 7 + (-x) - 3x^{2} \\\\\n- 6x^{2} + 3x^{2} &= 7 - x - 3x^{2} \\\\\nx &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if $x=7$, we get $y=-7$ and the corresponding point is $(7,-7)$."}]}
